    } Semax Dosage Chart for a 10 mg Vial This Semax dosage chart explains how a 10 mg vial can be calculated for intranasal research with a verified 0.10 mL metered actuator. Published human Semax studies primarily used intranasal drops or measured intranasal solution. A measured 5 mL final volume provides 200 mcg per spray, while a measured 10 mL final volume provides 100 mcg per spray. Primary human research route: Intranasal drops or measured intranasal solution. 5 mL final volume: 2 mg/mL, 200 mcg per verified 0.10 mL spray, and 50 nominal sprays. 10 mL final volume: 1 mg/mL, 100 mcg per verified 0.10 mL spray, and 100 nominal sprays. Device check: Confirm the pump output and follow its priming instructions before using the calculation. Research context: Published amounts varied by study population, formulation, and research objective. Semax is a synthetic heptapeptide based on the ACTH(4-10) sequence. Human research has evaluated intranasal Semax in healthy volunteers and in condition-specific neurological studies [1] [2] [3] . The calculations below use the entire 10 mg vial and keep published study protocols separate from worked preparation examples. Research context: For evidence on mechanisms, human and preclinical research, limitations, and safety, read Semax Peptide: Benefits, Uses, Side Effects, Dosage, and Research . Keep this chart close Open this dosage and reconstitution chart directly from your account. Sign in to save Dosing & Reconstitution Guide Semax 10 mg intranasal research calculations for 5 mL and 10 mL final volumes Semax 10 mg Nasal Spray Calculations Both options begin with 3.0 mL in the peptide vial. Transfer the complete solution, then add enough vehicle to reach the measured final volume. Measured Final Volume Vehicle Added After Transfer Final Concentration Per Verified 0.10 mL Spray 5.0 mL About 2.0 mL 2,000 mcg/mL 200 mcg 50 nominal sprays 10.0 mL About 7.0 mL 1,000 mcg/mL 100 mcg 100 nominal sprays Reference Schedule Period Amount Spray Volume Frequency Days 1–5 200–300 mcg per administration (400–900 mcg/day) 0.20–0.30 mL at 1 mg/mL (2–3 verified 0.10 mL sprays) 2–3 times in the first half of the day Research reference: This translates the registered 0.1% Semax drop schedule [4] into volume for a verified 0.10 mL actuator. A custom research solution and pump are not equivalent to the registered preserved formulation. Calculation basis: Each row uses the full 10 mg vial and a pump verified to release 0.10 mL per complete actuation. Nominal spray counts are calculated before priming and normal residual hold-up. How to Prepare the 5 mL or 10 mL Research Bottle Confirm the 10 mg vial, sterile research vehicle, 5 mL or 10 mL actuator bottle, and documented pump output. Add 3.0 mL of compatible sterile vehicle to the vial, then gently swirl until dissolved. Do not shake. Transfer the complete solution to the actuator bottle and add enough vehicle to reach exactly 5.0 mL or 10.0 mL . Secure and label the bottle, prime the pump as directed, then refrigerate at 4°C and protect from light; the supplier lists up to four weeks after reconstitution [8] . Vehicle context: The worked example can use sterile 0.9% saline as a research vehicle. Kaplan and colleagues reported physiological solution in one human experiment. The registered 0.1% Semax product used purified water with methylparaben, so the saline example is not the same as the registered multidose formulation [1] . Research Amount to Spray Equivalents Research Amount 5 mL Option 200 mcg per Spray 10 mL Option 100 mcg per Spray 200 mcg 1 spray 2 sprays 300 mcg 1.5 theoretical sprays 3 sprays 400 mcg 2 sprays 4 sprays 500 mcg 2.5 theoretical sprays 5 sprays These rows are concentration conversions. They do not turn one study amount into a universal schedule. Fractional results are mathematical only because a metered pump cannot deliver half of a full actuation. Use the measured final volume and verified actuator output in every calculation. Bottle capacity alone does not establish the final volume or the amount delivered per spray. Supplies Needed Use one complete set of measured and sterile research supplies for each prepared Semax 10 mg nasal bottle. Semax 10 mg research vial: One vial per prepared bottle. The product gallery below links to the existing Semax 10 mg supplier page [8] . Sterile research vehicle: Single-use sterile 0.9% sodium chloride for the worked saline example, or another validated sterile nasal research vehicle. Sterile nasal actuator bottle: One 5 mL or 10 mL bottle intended for single-user research. Metered pump: A compatible actuator documented or verified at 0.10 mL per complete spray. Calibrated transfer tools: Sterile syringes or pipettes that can measure the initial 3.0 mL and the exact 5.0 mL or 10.0 mL final volume. Sterile transfer needle: Required when the peptide vial uses a rubber septum. Clean handling supplies: Alcohol preparation pads, clean gloves, and a disinfected work surface. Label and research log: Record the peptide amount, final volume, concentration, pump output, preparation date, priming sprays, and actuation count. Storage materials: Light-protective refrigerated storage appropriate to the validated formulation. Sharps container: Use when transfer needles are part of the preparation workflow. View Supplier Research Protocol Overview Quick reference for the intranasal Semax 10 mg calculation. Human research route: Intranasal drops or solution. Peptide amount: 10,000 mcg total in the vial. 5 mL option: 2,000 mcg/mL and 200 mcg per verified 0.10 mL spray. 10 mL option: 1,000 mcg/mL and 100 mcg per verified 0.10 mL spray. Final-volume rule: Bring the transferred solution to an exact measured final volume. Research Amount Calculator Spray equivalents for a verified 0.10 mL actuator. 200 mcg: 1 spray at 5 mL or 2 sprays at 10 mL. 300 mcg: 1.5 theoretical sprays at 5 mL or 3 sprays at 10 mL. 400 mcg: 2 sprays at 5 mL or 4 sprays at 10 mL. 500 mcg: 2.5 theoretical sprays at 5 mL or 5 sprays at 10 mL. Interpretation: These are mathematical equivalents. Fractional values cannot be delivered as partial pump strokes. Match any research amount to its original study context. Storage Instructions Storage depends on the complete formulation, container, and handling method. Lyophilized vial: Store at -20°C and protect from light according to the supplier listing [8] . Prepared solution: Refrigerate at 4°C; the supplier lists up to four weeks after reconstitution [8] . Labeling: Record preparation date, final volume, concentration, vehicle, and storage conditions. Handling: Keep the cap secured and minimize unnecessary warming and light exposure. Published Human Intranasal Semax Dosage Research Human Semax research has primarily used intranasal drops or measured intranasal solution. The published amount cannot be separated from the study population, formulation, device, or research objective, so the rows below are evidence summaries rather than a universal Semax dosing recommendation. Source Research setting Published intranasal protocol Kaplan et al., 1996 [1] Healthy-volunteer performance and EEG experiments Experiments included 0.210 mg and 1 mg exposures; one operator experiment reported 1 mg in 0.4 mL physiological solution. Lebedeva et al., 2018 [2] Healthy-volunteer functional MRI study A single 1.2 mg exposure using a 1% solution, with 60 microliters placed into each nostril. Gusev et al., 2018 [3] Condition-specific ischemic-stroke research 6,000 mcg per day for 10 days, followed by a second 10-day course after a 20-day interval. Nasution et al., 2013 [10] Moderate head-injury study with standard care 9 mg on day 1, 6 mg on day 2, and 3 mg daily on days 3–5. Peptic-ulcer study [11] Semax added to conventional therapy A 1% solution, 2–4 drops three times daily for 10 days; the abstract does not establish a general-purpose protocol. Motor-neuron-disease study [12] Open-label, condition-specific research 12 mg per day in two 10-day courses separated by a two-week break. Evidence boundary: These studies differ substantially in population, concentration, duration, co-treatments, and outcomes. Their published amounts should not be combined into one “standard” schedule. Semax Nasal Spray Dosage Calculations A Semax nasal spray calculation requires two verified inputs: the measured final solution volume and the actuator output. Pharmaceutical literature reports pump volumes from about 0.025 mL to 0.20 mL per spray, so a nominal bottle size cannot establish the delivered amount [5] . Core Formula mcg per spray = (10,000 mcg ÷ final volume in mL) × verified mL per spray nominal sprays = final volume in mL ÷ verified mL per spray Verified actuator output 5 mL option 2,000 mcg/mL 10 mL option 1,000 mcg/mL 0.05 mL 100 mcg 50 mcg 0.10 mL 200 mcg 100 mcg 0.13 mL 260 mcg 130 mcg FDA nasal-product guidance emphasizes priming, dose uniformity, pump delivery, and performance testing because device behavior affects the amount released from each actuation [6] . What Is Semax Peptide? Semax is a synthetic peptide and heptapeptide with the amino-acid sequence Met-Glu-His-Phe-Pro-Gly-Pro. It was originally developed in Russia from the ACTH(4–10) fragment while avoiding the hormonal activity of full-length adrenocorticotropic hormone [7] . Human studies have evaluated intranasal Semax in healthy volunteers and condition-specific neurological settings, while much of the mechanistic literature remains preclinical. Research has examined brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF), neurotrophic signaling, dopaminergic pathways, gene expression, attention, cognitive function, cognitive performance, functional connectivity, and neuroprotection. Secondary sources sometimes describe Semax as a nootropic or discuss cognitive enhancement, brain health, and mental performance, but those phrases exceed what small, heterogeneous human studies can establish [1] [2] [7] . Human Findings and Evidence Limits Healthy-volunteer research: Small studies reported changes in attention, operator performance, EEG measures, or functional connectivity after intranasal exposure [1] [2] . Condition-specific research: Stroke, head-injury, peptic-ulcer, and motor-neuron-disease studies used very different protocols and often evaluated Semax alongside other care [3] [10] [11] [12] . Reported effects: Cognitive and neurological findings were measured with different endpoints, so results cannot be pooled into one claim about potential benefits. No universal dose-response curve: The literature does not establish that one intranasal amount, spray count, or cycle applies across research objectives. Formulation matters: A custom solution made from lyophilized material is not automatically equivalent to the preserved 0.1% or 1% nasal-drop products described in foreign literature. Safety and Regulatory Status Semax is not an FDA-approved drug in the United States. FDA’s current bulk-substance safety information identifies insufficient safety data and potential immunogenicity risks related to aggregation and peptide impurities for Semax prepared by compounding pharmacies [9] . Published and foreign-label sources describe nasal irritation among the potential side effects, but the available literature is too heterogeneous to define a complete adverse-effect profile or a favorable safety profile for custom nasal preparations. Research records should capture formulation, concentration, actuator, exposure, nasal condition, and unexpected observations. Research Controls That Affect Nasal Delivery Pump specification: Record the stated and independently checked mL per full actuation. Priming status: Document first-use priming, repriming, missed sprays, and tail-off near bottle exhaustion. Final volume: Measure the solution volume rather than relying on the bottle-capacity mark. Formulation: Record the vehicle, preservative status, peptide concentration, and container compatibility. Nasal condition: Document congestion, irritation, or other conditions that could change deposition on the nasal mucosa. Nasal Actuator Technique for Research Records Confirm the labeled concentration and verified actuator output. Prime or reprime the pump exactly as the device manufacturer specifies. Keep bottle orientation, stroke length, and actuation force consistent. Record every actuation, including priming sprays and incomplete strokes. Clean the nozzle with a device-compatible method, replace the cap, and return the bottle to its documented storage condition. Semax Storage and Stability Notes Pure Lab Peptides lists the lyophilized 10 mg vial for storage at -20°C and the reconstituted material at 4°C for up to four weeks, or -20°C for up to six months [8] . A custom multidose nasal preparation also depends on vehicle sterility, preservative status, container closure, and repeated pump use; the supplier’s peptide-stability statement does not by itself validate microbiological stability in every nasal bottle. Label the prepared bottle with the peptide mass, measured final volume, concentration, actuator output, preparation date, and storage condition. Protect it from light and avoid unnecessary warming. Semax Dosage FAQ How much Semax is in one 0.10 mL spray? With a 10 mg vial measured to 5 mL, one verified 0.10 mL spray contains 200 mcg. At a 10 mL final volume, the same verified spray contains 100 mcg. How many sprays are in a 10 mg Semax bottle? Theoretical counts are 50 sprays from 5 mL or 100 sprays from 10 mL when the actuator releases exactly 0.10 mL. Priming, residual volume, and pump tail-off reduce the usable count. Is intranasal Semax the same as injectable Semax? No. The human studies summarized here used intranasal administration. Route, formulation, absorption, and measurement differ, so intranasal amounts cannot be converted into an injection or subcutaneous injection protocol. Does research establish one Semax cycle length? No. A research peptide protocol should retain the duration and population from its source; the literature does not establish one universal cycle length. How long did published Semax protocols last? Published human protocols ranged from a single exposure to short multi-day courses and repeated condition-specific courses [2] [3] [10] [12] . Duration must remain attached to the original study context. Are Semax and N-acetyl Semax the same compound? No. Semax, N-acetyl Semax amidate, and other analogs have different structures and should not be treated as interchangeable in a dosage guide or calculation. Important Note This Semax dosage information is for educational and research purposes only . It is not medical advice and does not establish a universal human-use protocol. References Kaplan et al. (1996) Synthetic ACTH analogue Semax displays nootropic-like activity in humans. View Source Lebedeva et al. (2018) Effects of Semax on the default mode network of the brain. View Source Gusev et al. (2018) Efficacy of Semax in patients at different stages of ischemic stroke. View Source RLSnet Semax 0.1% product instructions based on the manufacturer-approved instruction. View Source Trows et al. (2014) Analytical challenges and regulatory requirements for nasal drug products. View Source U.S. Food and Drug Administration Nasal spray product guidance covering pump delivery, priming, dose uniformity, and performance. View Source Deigin et al. (2022) Development of peptide biopharmaceuticals in Russia. View Source Pure Lab Peptides Semax 10 mg research product details, batch documentation, and storage information. View Source U.S. Food and Drug Administration Safety information for certain bulk substances used in compounding, including Semax. View Source Nasution et al. (2013) ACTH(4-10)Pro8-Gly9-Pro10 in moderate head injury. View Source Therapy of peptic ulcer with Semax peptide (2002) Intranasal Semax added to conventional therapy. View Source Motor neuron disease Semax study (2008) Open-label intranasal research with two 10-day courses. V